{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":252240,"reputation":28968,"user_id":530160,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":69,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/xRnFS.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Nick ODell","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/530160/nick-odell"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1711930671,"post_id":78253195,"comment_id":137958376,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Can you include the original JSON?"},{"owner":{"account_id":13789219,"reputation":54663,"user_id":9952196,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/cd1a3f93172eb89e221c98e35e6e22b3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Shawn","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/9952196/shawn"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1711934265,"post_id":78253195,"comment_id":137958556,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Wrap it in <code>[]</code>&#39;s (and no need for the last <code>| .</code>? <code>jq &#39;[ .DailyForecasts[].Day.RainProbability ]&#39;</code> or something along those lines"},{"owner":{"account_id":1569642,"reputation":115,"user_id":1457613,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":43,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0c0223ebb4bd490d2c28a631da0f8cce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user1457613","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1457613/user1457613"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1711963115,"post_id":78253195,"comment_id":137960567,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thank you for that. I knew it was something simple. However, now I try to convert to bash array (the desired output). I only get the first value of the array."},{"owner":{"account_id":1569642,"reputation":115,"user_id":1457613,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":43,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0c0223ebb4bd490d2c28a631da0f8cce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"user1457613","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1457613/user1457613"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1711963864,"post_id":78253195,"comment_id":137960636,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"... When I try to convert to bash array I only get the first instance of array: &#39;2&#39; in this case. Same with string value (only the first word): &#39;Breezy&#39;. Ex: arr=($(echo $drp | jq -r &#39;.[]&#39;))  Same with mapfile, same with regular exp. I have modified post to include full json. Thank you so much."},{"owner":{"account_id":6313442,"reputation":46169,"user_id":5625547,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/oqCND.jpg?s=256","display_name":"0stone0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5625547/0stone0"},"edited":false,"score":4,"creation_date":1712069280,"post_id":78262117,"comment_id":137973668,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Jq is for Json, you&#39;ll need yq"},{"owner":{"account_id":26839391,"reputation":408,"user_id":20426148,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ALm5wu0TcHQFj-LEUsi2BcqGowziE67Lgi_E3LMObQ0=k-s256","display_name":"michaelt","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/20426148/michaelt"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6313442,"reputation":46169,"user_id":5625547,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/oqCND.jpg?s=256","display_name":"0stone0","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/5625547/0stone0"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1712075726,"post_id":78262117,"comment_id":137974824,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@0stone0 is right.  yq is for YAML (which is what you&#39;re wokring with)"},{"owner":{"account_id":466379,"reputation":17770,"user_id":871096,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":52,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/39b6e181c797ef3ab2119322f7cac20b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Robert Dodier","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/871096/robert-dodier"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1712078935,"post_id":78260959,"comment_id":137975317,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Thanks for the note about grid tables, that&#39;s just the effect I wanted. I think I&#39;ll struggle onward with what I have already since it&#39;s more or less working, but I&#39;ll use that for the next one. About LaTeX tables, yeah, I go back and forth between Markdown and LaTeX ... sometimes I can get what I want from Markdown, sometimes I decide it&#39;s just easier to go straight to LaTeX. For the most part, pandoc guesses defaults for stuff which works really well for me; there is just a little bit of stuff around the edges that needs adjustment."},{"owner":{"account_id":316835,"reputation":120015,"user_id":997358,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f849cfde4e526e22e916fa69103ef7e7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"peak","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/997358/peak"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1712083547,"post_id":78262117,"comment_id":137976008,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"..… or gojq ….."},{"owner":{"account_id":2478374,"reputation":38977,"user_id":2158479,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b5f80e7aa8246b07b48602dfa049281c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"pmf","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2158479/pmf"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1712105457,"post_id":78262117,"comment_id":137978485,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"(Modern) YAML processors (like yq) can always process JSON data because the JSON format is a subset of the (modern) YAML format. But the other way round, a JSON processor (like jq) cannot process (non-JSON) YAML data unless it has special capabilities to do so (like gojq, which simply has both processors implemented)."},{"owner":{"account_id":2821493,"reputation":23453,"user_id":2425163,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":75,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/05f4000597865eeaf51c32ed5694479c?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"tarleb","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2425163/tarleb"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1712146561,"post_id":78267487,"comment_id":137983221,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Is there any chance to get the formulas in a different format? Which software was used to generate the HTML?  I believe the only way would be to make pandoc handle this as math would be to hand-code a converter from that format to MathML."},{"owner":{"account_id":26215692,"reputation":69,"user_id":19893853,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AFdZucqqrK58PkBCrf8pFpL8TcdylI6JmWF9J_V0UCPJ9Q=k-s256","display_name":"Alexey Bogdanov","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/19893853/alexey-bogdanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1712162236,"post_id":78269109,"comment_id":137986143,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Yes in my scenario manual way of converting is not OK because we have a lot of formulas. I put here only one very simple example. Thank you for your tip I will try to use this converter. I already have experience in converting from markdown to asciidoc so if this tool can handle this formula syntax and give me markdown output it will be quite OK for my task."},{"owner":{"account_id":26215692,"reputation":69,"user_id":19893853,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AFdZucqqrK58PkBCrf8pFpL8TcdylI6JmWF9J_V0UCPJ9Q=k-s256","display_name":"Alexey Bogdanov","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/19893853/alexey-bogdanov"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1712164290,"post_id":78269109,"comment_id":137986449,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"That is really good converter (from html to markdown) but still the output lacks any wrappers around formulas which is not good. If I convert further from markdown to asciidoc I just cannot add stem-block around formulas because they are not distinguished from regular text :((  If anybody know a converter which can do this and also wrap the formulas I would appreciate the link to it."},{"owner":{"account_id":15044810,"reputation":21078,"user_id":10858321,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/4h8dvAxL.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Shafee","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/10858321/shafee"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1715403059,"post_id":78461996,"comment_id":138328053,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Keep an eye on the <a href=\"https://github.com/quarto-dev/quarto-cli/issues/341\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>issue #341</code></a> on the quarto github repository asking for the output folding feature. <b>Note that, the workaround proposed in that issue thread, will work only for R code cell, not python code cell</b>."},{"owner":{"account_id":1938048,"reputation":210640,"user_id":1745001,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/99a3ebae89496eb16afe453aae97f5be?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Ed Morton","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/1745001/ed-morton"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1717347751,"post_id":78253195,"comment_id":138511257,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<code>arr=($(echo $drp | jq -r &#39;.[]&#39;))</code> has multiple problems and is fundamentally not the way to populate a shell array with the output of a command, ask a new question about that if you&#39;d like help with it."},{"owner":{"account_id":3577221,"reputation":3866,"user_id":2986153,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/PtaWg.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/2986153/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1723575864,"post_id":78854242,"comment_id":139051384,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"where would such code be placed? Clearly it&#39;s not a chunk option."},{"owner":{"account_id":18297881,"reputation":345,"user_id":13323413,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/AOh14GiMxs6sydbRJTmnM8uYgex26xoE3wrVy4EBnp4vjw0=k-s256","display_name":"Md Ahsanul Himel","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/13323413/md-ahsanul-himel"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1728212583,"post_id":78854242,"comment_id":139400133,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Did you find the solution?"},{"owner":{"account_id":15351890,"reputation":325,"user_id":12118669,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/dbf2d6df0d84c24a647820f6f479d233?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"loreabad6","link":"https://stackoverflow.com/users/12118669/loreabad6"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1742988459,"post_id":78854242,"comment_id":140265140,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The answer is in the linked picture, if you click on collapse=true"}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":225}